  <adoption-note date="2026-08-28">
    ADOPTED INTO THIS PROJECT, NOT DEFERRED. Owner direction 2026-08-28: "if an issue is
    important, then it should be added to this project NOT deferred to others."

    ADR-038 is this project OWN ADR (Stream B). PR #865 armed B4 and B1 because both were at zero live instances. The other 15 remain documented-but-unenforced, which is the gap #864 was filed about. Leaving 15 of 17 as prose is leaving the deliverable half-built.

    This is a DEFECT REPORT AGAINST THIS PROJECT OWN DELIVERABLE, not new scope. The project
    cannot honestly close while something it shipped is known-broken. That is north star #1
    ("fix it right the first time") and #3 ("no constant reds").
  </adoption-note>

  <goal>Every ADR-038 ban is either enforced by a blocking guard, or carries a written reason why it cannot be, with the live count that makes the case.</goal>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="1">For each of B2, B3, B5-B17, measure the live count using the /test-diet classifier (hardened over six spot-check rounds: comment-stripped matching, deny-list assertions, helper-aware, literal-safe brace counting, expression-bodied capture).</step>
    <step order="2">Classify each: (a) at zero, arm a hard guard now at no migration cost; (b) small non-zero, propose a migration or a reasoned inventory; (c) not mechanically detectable, document WHY and hand to /test-diet judgement.</step>
    <step order="3">B6 (mirror tests) is expected to land in (c): implementation == implementation has no regex signature, and it is plausibly the largest real bucket. Say so plainly rather than shipping a weak detector.</step>
    <step order="4">B13 (~1,124 live) lands in (b), and the owner has already ruled that a bad name is NOT grounds for deletion. The only honest options are rename-over-time via /test-diet, or no guard. Do not re-litigate that decision.</step>
    <step order="5">ARM IN THE SAME PR. Per #839 an unarmed guard is a file that merely looks like enforcement. Verify with the exact Tier 1 filter STRING, not just the test class name.</step>
    <step order="6">Every new guard carries a negative control (fires on the real defect) and a positive control (ignores the sanctioned shape), per the tests/CLAUDE.md structural-fitness rules.</step>
  </steps>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">All 17 bans accounted for: enforced, or documented-unenforceable with a live count</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every new guard is armed in the Tier 1 filter and verified with the exact filter string</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every new guard has both a negative and a positive control</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No guard ships red</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>
</task>
